            <ArticleTitle>Psychological and behavioral dynamics in chronic atypical facial pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>252-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The authors discuss the relationship between atypical facial pain and psychiatric disturbance. They present contemporary viewpoints and describe four cases that illustrate underlying psychodynamic mechanisms associated with pain in patients who had undergone various dental procedures and other treatments without success. They identify factors which might lead to the early detection of underlying psychological problems and discuss the role of learning, the family system and other factors in producing a chronic pain syndrome.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
